From e0f559713b90a77510c2c62fecffd7d5705151de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Davanum Srinivas <davanum@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 17:57:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] agent: kill the init process and child cgroups of a
 container

A SIGKILL for a container with an empty exec ID tells the agent to
signal all the processes of the container. Since #13163 the agent
signals only the PIDs that the cgroup manager returns. It no longer
signals the init process directly.

get_pids() of the cgroupfs manager, which the systemd manager also
uses, reads the processes of the container cgroup only. With cgroup v2,
a cgroup that enables controllers for its children cannot hold
processes. systemd and nested container runtimes therefore move every
process of the container, PID 1 included, into child cgroups. The list
is then empty. The agent signals nothing and returns success, so
`ctr tasks kill -s SIGKILL`, the kill at the end of `crictl stop` and
a pod delete have no effect on such a container.

Fix both parts:

- get_pids() returns the processes of the container cgroup and of all
  its descendant cgroups. It reads cgroup.procs, which exists with
  cgroup v1 and v2 and lists processes, not threads. PID 0, which the
  kernel prints for a process outside of the PID namespace of the
  reader, is never returned. LinuxContainer::destroy() uses the same
  list, so it now also finds processes in child cgroups of a container
  that shares the PID namespace of the sandbox.

- The agent adds the init process to the PIDs that it signals, unless
  the process is already reaped. When the container has its own PID
  namespace, the kernel then kills all its other processes. This does
  not depend on the cgroup that a process is in.

Fixes: #14062
Signed-off-by: Davanum Srinivas <davanum@gmail.com>
